WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.196 --> 00:00:04.411
- Take big risks.

00:00:04.411 --> 00:00:05.507
- Take that risk.

00:00:05.507 --> 00:00:06.340
- Take risks.

00:00:06.340 --> 00:00:07.173
- Take risk.

00:00:07.173 --> 00:00:08.006
- Taking risks.

00:00:08.006 --> 00:00:08.839
- Take risks.

00:00:08.839 --> 00:00:09.672
- Risk taking.

00:00:09.672 --> 00:00:10.505
- Risky.

00:00:10.505 --> 00:00:11.338
- Take risks.

00:00:11.338 --> 00:00:12.661
- Hello Believe Nation,
today we're going to

00:00:12.661 --> 00:00:15.259
talk about how you can take risks.

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:17.441
And as always if you hear
something that is really relevant

00:00:17.441 --> 00:00:19.856
to you, that you love
the message of, please

00:00:19.856 --> 00:00:21.754
leave it in the comments
below and put quotes

00:00:21.754 --> 00:00:24.343
around it so other people
can be inspired as well.

00:00:24.343 --> 00:00:25.995
Enjoy.

00:00:25.995 --> 00:00:28.828
(classical music)

00:00:37.096 --> 00:00:39.625
- Just because you're
doing something and you're

00:00:39.625 --> 00:00:42.287
successful at it does not mean you have to

00:00:42.287 --> 00:00:46.232
continue doing it and in
some ways your willingness

00:00:46.232 --> 00:00:50.399
to take big risks defines
where your life is going to go.

00:00:52.561 --> 00:00:53.856
It's like right now, I got a great house

00:00:53.856 --> 00:00:55.423
and I got this cool stuff.

00:00:55.423 --> 00:00:58.895
If the right opportunity
comes up, am I willing

00:00:58.895 --> 00:01:01.385
to leverage all that to bet again?

00:01:01.385 --> 00:01:04.271
Your willingness to take those risks,

00:01:04.271 --> 00:01:07.809
especially when you're young
is the difference between

00:01:07.809 --> 00:01:11.778
an average life and an extraordinary life.

00:01:11.778 --> 00:01:15.260
- But I took this big
risk and quit school.

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:18.510
I quit the one thing that was sure-fire

00:01:19.643 --> 00:01:23.810
and became a model and got
really bored really fast.

00:01:27.225 --> 00:01:28.609
I didn't love being a
coat hanger and then I

00:01:28.609 --> 00:01:32.452
became an actor, which was
like a coat hanger that talks.

00:01:32.452 --> 00:01:33.740
(laughs)

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:37.652
And then I ultimately skipped full loop

00:01:37.652 --> 00:01:40.164
and came back around and by the way when

00:01:40.164 --> 00:01:41.931
you talk you're generally
not speaking your own

00:01:41.931 --> 00:01:44.640
words you're speaking
somebody else's words

00:01:44.640 --> 00:01:46.117
and trying to make it sound really real.

00:01:46.117 --> 00:01:48.356
And then I did full loop around

00:01:48.356 --> 00:01:51.771
and started looking at technology again.

00:01:51.771 --> 00:01:53.646
So I started getting
interested in technology

00:01:53.646 --> 00:01:55.341
and started getting
interested in engineering.

00:01:55.341 --> 00:01:59.565
And started really getting
interested in solving problems.

00:01:59.565 --> 00:02:01.708
Solving my own problems

00:02:01.708 --> 00:02:03.533
and then solving other people's problems.

00:02:03.533 --> 00:02:05.007
And what the value of that is.

00:02:05.007 --> 00:02:08.337
Whether it's technology or
philanthropy or actually

00:02:08.337 --> 00:02:11.486
even performing or being an actor.

00:02:11.486 --> 00:02:15.117
I started to make it
about solving problems

00:02:15.117 --> 00:02:19.454
and I think that's probably
the single biggest thing

00:02:19.454 --> 00:02:22.149
that as an Angel investor
I look for in entrepreneurs

00:02:22.149 --> 00:02:26.629
is people who genuinely
want to solve a problem.

00:02:26.629 --> 00:02:27.670
A real problem.

00:02:27.670 --> 00:02:29.247
A problem that exists not just for

00:02:29.247 --> 00:02:31.414
themselves but sometimes
just for themselves

00:02:31.414 --> 00:02:34.535
and then it sort of turns
into this wave effect

00:02:34.535 --> 00:02:37.184
that solves other people's problems.

00:02:37.184 --> 00:02:39.679
And sometimes by trying
to solve your own problem,

00:02:39.679 --> 00:02:42.801
you're solving everybody else's problems.

00:02:42.801 --> 00:02:44.735
Because generally if you
want to affect the world

00:02:44.735 --> 00:02:47.465
you have to change
yourself first, everybody

00:02:47.465 --> 00:02:49.276
tries to go out and change
the world by changing

00:02:49.276 --> 00:02:51.389
other people and other things but the

00:02:51.389 --> 00:02:54.048
real way to do it is by changing yourself.

00:02:54.048 --> 00:02:56.741
And sometimes making
those really uncomfortable

00:02:56.741 --> 00:02:59.168
choices to go up against
everyone in your life

00:02:59.168 --> 00:03:02.245
that you look up to and take that risk

00:03:02.245 --> 00:03:03.662
to go be a model.

00:03:06.511 --> 00:03:09.112
Or go do this thing that
nobody else is doing.

00:03:09.112 --> 00:03:12.067
- People told me that Black
Swan was an artistic risk.

00:03:12.067 --> 00:03:14.969
A scary challenge to try
to portray a professional

00:03:14.969 --> 00:03:17.146
ballet dancer but it didn't feel like

00:03:17.146 --> 00:03:19.698
courage or daring that drew me to it.

00:03:19.698 --> 00:03:21.932
I was so oblivious to my own limits that

00:03:21.932 --> 00:03:25.227
I did things I was
woefully unprepared to do.

00:03:25.227 --> 00:03:27.467
And so the very
inexperience that in college

00:03:27.467 --> 00:03:29.963
had made me feel insecure
and made me want to

00:03:29.963 --> 00:03:33.058
play by other's rules now
was making me actually

00:03:33.058 --> 00:03:36.627
take risks, I didn't
even realize were risks.

00:03:36.627 --> 00:03:38.530
When Darren asked me if I could do ballet,

00:03:38.530 --> 00:03:40.875
I told him that I was
basically a ballerina.

00:03:40.875 --> 00:03:43.762
Which, by the way, I
whole heartedly believed.

00:03:43.762 --> 00:03:46.386
When it quickly became
clear in preparing for

00:03:46.386 --> 00:03:48.654
the film that I was maybe 15 years away

00:03:48.654 --> 00:03:50.429
from being a ballerina.

00:03:50.429 --> 00:03:52.510
It made me work a million times harder

00:03:52.510 --> 00:03:54.342
and of course the magic of cinema

00:03:54.342 --> 00:03:57.031
and body doubles helped the final effect.

00:03:57.031 --> 00:03:59.701
But the point is, if I had
known my own limitations

00:03:59.701 --> 00:04:02.301
I never would have taken the risk and the

00:04:02.301 --> 00:04:03.944
risk lead to one of my greatest artistic

00:04:03.944 --> 00:04:06.877
and personal experiences
in that I not only felt

00:04:06.877 --> 00:04:10.300
completely free, I also met
my husband during filming.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.276
- When I visit students, I also tell them

00:04:12.276 --> 00:04:14.725
that the greatest risk they
take is not taking one.

00:04:14.725 --> 00:04:17.338
Which, by the way, Miss
Martinez taught me that.

00:04:17.338 --> 00:04:19.211
And I use it to this day.

00:04:19.211 --> 00:04:21.628
Anytime I feel that should I?

00:04:23.983 --> 00:04:27.520
I feel nervous, like I feel right now.

00:04:27.520 --> 00:04:28.853
I take the risk.

00:04:30.687 --> 00:04:32.837
And for all the students,
for all the teachers

00:04:32.837 --> 00:04:37.287
and for all the educators
here, we have to take risk.

00:04:37.287 --> 00:04:38.960
If we don't take that
risk, I go back to it.

00:04:38.960 --> 00:04:40.887
It's the biggest risk we take.

00:04:40.887 --> 00:04:45.054
- You'll have many opportunities
to reinvent yourself.

00:04:46.160 --> 00:04:49.493
So I think it's worthwhile taking risks,

00:04:50.844 --> 00:04:54.302
trying to do something
you're really excited by

00:04:54.302 --> 00:04:56.072
and if the first attempt
you don't do it you

00:04:56.072 --> 00:04:58.122
can try again and you
know things tend to work

00:04:58.122 --> 00:04:59.416
out in the long run.

00:04:59.416 --> 00:05:02.118
In Silicon Valley you know is in poverty

00:05:02.118 --> 00:05:04.823
and so many people start up a company.

00:05:04.823 --> 00:05:06.454
You know, starting up a
company and even having

00:05:06.454 --> 00:05:08.775
failed you know you can wear
it like a badge of honor.

00:05:08.775 --> 00:05:09.608
Right?

00:05:09.608 --> 00:05:11.258
And I think that's important, you know

00:05:11.258 --> 00:05:13.591
culturally risk is rewarded.

00:05:14.471 --> 00:05:16.675
I remember when I started
working at Google,

00:05:16.675 --> 00:05:19.672
if I went and people are discussing ideas.

00:05:19.672 --> 00:05:22.318
The other people who
heard the idea and started

00:05:22.318 --> 00:05:24.648
building on those ideas,
they encourage you.

00:05:24.648 --> 00:05:26.099
So it's a culture of optimism, it's a

00:05:26.099 --> 00:05:29.248
culture of risk taking and I
think that's really important.

00:05:29.248 --> 00:05:32.792
- I think that you have to
take risks all the time.

00:05:32.792 --> 00:05:36.280
If you don't do that,
you don't move forward.

00:05:36.280 --> 00:05:38.624
We are trying to be too conservative in

00:05:38.624 --> 00:05:40.808
the way that you think life.

00:05:40.808 --> 00:05:43.038
And the results is that
nobody is the first

00:05:43.038 --> 00:05:44.970
up to say I don't agree with this,

00:05:44.970 --> 00:05:49.137
I agree with that so we have
to push our individuality.

00:05:52.285 --> 00:05:56.221
To make sure that our voice can be heard.

00:05:56.221 --> 00:05:59.286
- There was one time, and
I don't even remember it.

00:05:59.286 --> 00:06:03.175
Might have been the Fantastic
Four or some other book.

00:06:03.175 --> 00:06:06.705
We had a story that I
thought wasn't that good.

00:06:06.705 --> 00:06:08.353
But we were stuck with it.

00:06:08.353 --> 00:06:10.594
We had to send it out,
it was the due date.

00:06:10.594 --> 00:06:11.985
I didn't have time to do it over.

00:06:11.985 --> 00:06:14.456
And on the cover I wrote a little note,

00:06:14.456 --> 00:06:16.399
I don't remember the exact
words but something like

00:06:16.399 --> 00:06:17.232
hey kids!

00:06:18.077 --> 00:06:20.254
I've got to admit this
isn't the best story

00:06:20.254 --> 00:06:23.400
we've ever done, but we've
given you so many good

00:06:23.400 --> 00:06:27.742
ones before we think you owe
it to us to buy this one also.

00:06:27.742 --> 00:06:29.600
Everybody thought I was crazy.

00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.767
That book sold better than most
of the others in that period

00:06:34.689 --> 00:06:38.104
and I got a ton of mail
saying you guys are great!

00:06:38.104 --> 00:06:40.943
Nobody else would have
written anything like that!

00:06:40.943 --> 00:06:43.688
- I don't know now a days if rejection is

00:06:43.688 --> 00:06:46.585
part of your every day life.

00:06:46.585 --> 00:06:48.785
So, I think a lot of
business in doing things

00:06:48.785 --> 00:06:51.512
is just the idea of having resilience.

00:06:51.512 --> 00:06:54.095
I'm going to ask you all a question.

00:06:55.519 --> 00:06:57.127
If your little five year old comes to you

00:06:57.127 --> 00:07:01.190
with a painting from school,
what are you going to say?

00:07:01.190 --> 00:07:02.073
- [Audience Member] Good job!

00:07:02.073 --> 00:07:04.673
- Absolutely the worst thing you can say.

00:07:04.673 --> 00:07:06.959
Because what you do by
saying good job is you

00:07:06.959 --> 00:07:08.932
teach a child to please others rather

00:07:08.932 --> 00:07:10.540
than please themselves.

00:07:10.540 --> 00:07:12.241
What you might want to
say is tell me about

00:07:12.241 --> 00:07:14.282
the painting and why did you do it.

00:07:14.282 --> 00:07:18.032
I don't know if today
people are risk takers.

00:07:21.945 --> 00:07:23.944
They want a lot of atta boys.

00:07:23.944 --> 00:07:26.527
You have to get it from
within sooner or later.

00:07:26.527 --> 00:07:27.860
So I'm not sure.

00:07:29.424 --> 00:07:30.695
Can I amplify a little bit?

00:07:30.695 --> 00:07:31.528
- Absolutely, please.

00:07:31.528 --> 00:07:35.618
- Here I am, I'm at UFC and
it's really not complicated.

00:07:35.618 --> 00:07:39.577
I saw all these people in line, Xeroxing.

00:07:39.577 --> 00:07:40.802
What a discovery.

00:07:40.802 --> 00:07:42.646
If they're Xeroxing at UFC why wouldn't

00:07:42.646 --> 00:07:44.122
they Xerox at Santa Barbara?

00:07:44.122 --> 00:07:45.497
Was this?

00:07:45.497 --> 00:07:47.331
Such a brief search.

00:07:47.331 --> 00:07:48.569
I didn't invent the Xerox machine.

00:07:48.569 --> 00:07:51.698
They were just in line Xeroxing.

00:07:51.698 --> 00:07:55.365
And at one point all
the customers could see

00:07:56.411 --> 00:07:57.717
I had all the customers in line.

00:07:57.717 --> 00:08:00.485
My own workers could see how
much money I was taking in.

00:08:00.485 --> 00:08:03.926
We expanded every college in America.

00:08:03.926 --> 00:08:06.966
What always baffled me
is why I didn't have

00:08:06.966 --> 00:08:10.237
more people across the
street competing with me.

00:08:10.237 --> 00:08:14.399
And I think even back then
we weren't risk takers.

00:08:14.399 --> 00:08:17.316
But look back on your grandparents.

00:08:18.718 --> 00:08:20.940
If your grandparents had seen

00:08:20.940 --> 00:08:22.171
that I had something good with customers

00:08:22.171 --> 00:08:23.443
in line, you think they would've been

00:08:23.443 --> 00:08:26.460
out there competing
with me in a heartbeat?

00:08:26.460 --> 00:08:30.627
So I'm not sure risk taking is
as rewarded as it should be.

00:08:32.143 --> 00:08:34.484
- You had pretty ambitious sales targets.

00:08:34.484 --> 00:08:35.790
Are you going to be able to hit those?

00:08:35.790 --> 00:08:37.466
- You never know until the last minute.

00:08:37.466 --> 00:08:38.299
You know?

00:08:38.299 --> 00:08:40.632
The objective of when
you make a long term plan

00:08:40.632 --> 00:08:42.237
is not to make sure, from the beginning,

00:08:42.237 --> 00:08:44.061
that you're going to hit
that because if you are

00:08:44.061 --> 00:08:45.218
sure from the beginning
you're going to hit all

00:08:45.218 --> 00:08:47.795
your targets that means your
targets are conservative.

00:08:47.795 --> 00:08:49.773
That's the only way you can be sure.

00:08:49.773 --> 00:08:52.828
So you want to maintain
a certain stretch inside

00:08:52.828 --> 00:08:56.308
the company and be innovative and creative

00:08:56.308 --> 00:08:58.836
and finding new products and new solutions

00:08:58.836 --> 00:09:00.164
in order to hit the target.

00:09:00.164 --> 00:09:04.659
So it's risky but that's the
price to pay for the company.

00:09:04.659 --> 00:09:08.821
- Almost any accomplishment
in the history of the world

00:09:08.821 --> 00:09:12.452
has come at the hands of the
people who have taken risk.

00:09:12.452 --> 00:09:15.113
People who ar willing
to push the envelope.

00:09:15.113 --> 00:09:18.242
People who are willing
to explore the unknown.

00:09:18.242 --> 00:09:21.380
Even if you go and you look in the Bible.

00:09:21.380 --> 00:09:24.637
The people that are held up as the heroes

00:09:24.637 --> 00:09:25.584
they're not the ones who were

00:09:25.584 --> 00:09:27.619
sitting in a tent under an olive tree.

00:09:27.619 --> 00:09:28.452
You know?

00:09:28.452 --> 00:09:29.297
They were people who were

00:09:29.297 --> 00:09:31.162
going out there and doing stuff.

00:09:31.162 --> 00:09:36.114
And you know the key thing
about taking risk is learning.

00:09:36.114 --> 00:09:39.259
And if something doesn't
work out, learn from it.

00:09:39.259 --> 00:09:41.076
And then you're able to move further.

00:09:41.076 --> 00:09:44.827
We have to always recognize that knowledge

00:09:44.827 --> 00:09:46.979
frequently comes at a price.

00:09:46.979 --> 00:09:51.578
Thomas Edison said 999 ways
that a light bulb did not work.

00:09:51.578 --> 00:09:55.815
Many of us are familiar with
the cleaning formula 409

00:09:55.815 --> 00:09:56.971
Why do they call it that?

00:09:56.971 --> 00:09:59.268
The first 408 didn't work.

00:09:59.268 --> 00:10:01.748
You just put these things into perspective

00:10:01.748 --> 00:10:02.770
and you learn from them.

00:10:02.770 --> 00:10:05.876
Walter Dandy, the great
neurosurgeon at John Hopkins

00:10:05.876 --> 00:10:08.749
many decades ago, was the
first one to do operations

00:10:08.749 --> 00:10:11.147
on the posterior fossa,
the back part of the brain.

00:10:11.147 --> 00:10:13.457
No one thought it could be done.

00:10:13.457 --> 00:10:15.707
First 13 patients all died.

00:10:16.923 --> 00:10:19.460
Now can you imagine how
devastating that was?

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:21.811
I can't even imagine
what he said to the 14th

00:10:21.811 --> 00:10:24.533
patient when he said how the other 13 did.

00:10:24.533 --> 00:10:28.068
But the fact of the
matter is we now are able

00:10:28.068 --> 00:10:32.353
to do that operation routinely and safely.

00:10:32.353 --> 00:10:33.842
I did one last week.

00:10:33.842 --> 00:10:38.719
So, we just have to put all
these things in perspective

00:10:38.719 --> 00:10:42.752
and recognize that we
will never make progress

00:10:42.752 --> 00:10:45.019
unless we're willing to take risk.

00:10:45.019 --> 00:10:48.946
But, we have to take them
in an appropriate fashion.

00:10:48.946 --> 00:10:49.779
- [Host] Right.

00:10:49.779 --> 00:10:52.873
- Because there's a
large group of people who

00:10:52.873 --> 00:10:55.164
never get anywhere because
they're afraid to take a risk.

00:10:55.164 --> 00:10:57.953
There's another group of
people who never get anywhere

00:10:57.953 --> 00:11:00.018
because they take too
many of the wrong risks.

00:11:00.018 --> 00:11:02.567
They're always getting
knocked back into the pond.

00:11:02.567 --> 00:11:05.694
So that's where this
tremendous brain comes in

00:11:05.694 --> 00:11:10.485
where you are able to analyze
the appropriate risks.

00:11:10.485 --> 00:11:11.620
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:11:11.620 --> 00:11:12.622
I hope you enjoyed.

00:11:12.622 --> 00:11:14.358
I'd love to know what
you thought of the video.

00:11:14.358 --> 00:11:15.515
What was your favorite clip and why?

00:11:15.515 --> 00:11:19.300
How are you taking risks in your business?

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:20.885
Leave in the comments down below.

00:11:20.885 --> 00:11:22.371
I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:11:22.371 --> 00:11:23.797
I also want to give a quick shout out to

00:11:23.797 --> 00:11:25.533
William Chilton, William
thank you so much for

00:11:25.533 --> 00:11:27.473
picking up a copy of
my book Your One Word.

00:11:27.473 --> 00:11:29.912
And for posting the
picture, I love it man.

00:11:29.912 --> 00:11:31.363
And I really really appreciate it.

00:11:31.363 --> 00:11:32.392
So thank you guys again for watching.

00:11:32.392 --> 00:11:33.784
I believe in you.

00:11:33.784 --> 00:11:35.063
I hope you continue to believe

00:11:35.063 --> 00:11:38.384
in yourself and whatever your one word is.

00:11:38.384 --> 00:11:39.689
Much love.

00:11:39.689 --> 00:11:41.189
I'll see you soon.

